  <section id="what" class="sec prose" data-glyph="何">
    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>
      <em>Knots of Existence</em> is a long-running, open visualisation of a question: what, physically, is the thing a foam roller finds
      and a slow exhale releases? It is built around the essay{' '}
      <a href="https://ejhong.substack.com/p/knots-of-existence-hypotheses">Knots of Existence Hypotheses</a> (Eugene Jhong, 2026), its
      predecessor <a href="https://ejhong.substack.com/p/knots-of-existence-and-vasocomputation">Knots of Existence and Vasocomputation</a>,
      and the other answers the literature and the traditions offer. It is not medical advice, and nothing here is a protocol.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="measured" class="sec" data-glyph="測">
    <h2>What is measured, and what is representative</h2>
    <dl class="kv">
      <dt>Body</dt>
      <dd>
        The MakeHuman base mesh and age targets (CC0), averaged across its ethnic targets, from infancy to ninety; male by default, with a
        form control in the atlas settings that blends to female. Smoothed by one level of Catmull–Clark subdivision at runtime.
      </dd>
      <dt>Perforators</dt>
      <dd>
        Counts come from the literature: an average of 374 major perforators (≥ 0.5 mm) per body in about forty source-artery territories
        (Taylor &amp; Palmer, 1987), and on the order of a hundred thousand small perforators at 4–5 mm spacing (an estimate from skin
        area). Major perforators are placed more densely where surgeons find them clustered — the paraspinal and parasternal rows, the
        periumbilical field, the anterolateral thigh, near joints — and the rest by blue-noise sampling. <strong>Positions are
        representative, not a map of any one person</strong>: real perforators vary by centimetres between people and between sides.
      </dd>
      <dt>Fascia</dt>
      <dd>
        The superficial and deep fascia sit at typical depths for each region — thin over the scalp, face, hands and feet; thickest over the
        abdomen and buttocks. The exploded view opens the gliding plane and the fat by fixed gaps, so the perforators crossing them can be
        seen; the depth control returns the layers to anatomical depths. The deep channels — intermuscular septa, raphes and the sheaths of
        vessels and nerves — are drawn along their landmarks.
      </dd>
      <dt>Trees</dt>
      <dd>
        Each perforator joins its nearest larger vessel, and each major perforator its root, by shortest paths along the skin. Real source
        vessels run beneath the deep fascia; the trees are drawn on the surface for legibility.
      </dd>
      <dt>Knots</dt>
      <dd>
        The model illustrates the theories; it is not a measurement. On the perforator view each site carries a vessel tone, a collar gel and
        a nerve state, coupled as that hypothesis describes, with timescales compressed so a release can be watched. Knots gather where people
        report them — neck and shoulders, the edges of the shoulder blades, low back and hips, jaw and belly — and grow in number with age
        along a predicted curve: a census no one has yet taken. The vascular latch is drawn inside muscle in the same places, on the same
        curve, so the two can be compared. Every theory’s knots are drawn the same way, in the same colour; only their place differs.
      </dd>
      <dt>Maps</dt>
      <dd>
        Traditional maps are drawn from standard sources (for acupoints, the WHO Western Pacific standard) and placed by anatomical landmarks
        on this body. They are approximate and for comparison, not for needling.
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section id="moderation" class="sec prose" data-glyph="中">
    <h2>Moderation</h2>
    <p>
      Go gently. Deep somatic work and intensive meditation carry real dangers, and people have been harmed by doing too much, too fast —
      forceful breathing and breath-holds, hard pressure held too long, long retreats with little sleep. Difficulties can last months or
      years. Never press hard into the neck, where the arteries to the brain lie close beneath the surface. Nothing here is a protocol:
      work in moderation, rest often, and stop when something feels wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are struggling, you are not alone. The{' '}
      <a href="https://sites.brown.edu/britton/research/the-varieties-of-contemplative-experience/">Varieties of Contemplative Experience</a>{' '}
      project at Brown documents meditation-related difficulties, and <a href="https://www.cheetahhouse.org">Cheetah House</a> supports
      meditators in distress. For anything physical, see a physician.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="open" class="sec prose" data-glyph="開">
    <h2>Open source</h2>
    <p>
      The code is MIT-licensed and the writing and data are CC BY 4.0, at{' '}
      <a href="https://github.com/ejhong/knots">github.com/ejhong/knots</a>. Hypotheses, maps and references are plain data files; new maps are
      placed by anatomical landmarks rather than coordinates. Corrections from anatomists, surgeons, clinicians and practitioners of the
      traditions are especially welcome. The look follows <a href="https://ejhong.github.io/om/">The OM Project</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="mono small">Three.js · Astro · MakeHuman (CC0) · system type: Georgia, SF Mono</p>
  </section>
